Debugging a hanging AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service) job requires a systematic approach. Here's how to troubleshoot and resolve jobs that remain stuck in the running state:
1. Check the Job and Pod Status
First, get detailed information about the job:
bash: kubectl get job <job-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml
kubectl describe job <job-name> -n <namespace>
Then check the associated pods:
bash: kubectl get pods -n <namespace> | grep <job-name>
kubectl describe pod <job-name-pod-id> -n <namespace>
2. Examine Pod Logs
Check the logs of the running pod to see what it's doing:
bash kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
# For previous logs if the container has restarted
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

To check whether container has restarted:
Get Detailed Pod Information
bash kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
In the output, look for:

The "Restart Count" field for each container
The "Last State" section which shows information about the previous container instance
"State" and "Ready" status, which can indicate current container health

3. Look for Resource Constraints
Check if the job is waiting for resources:

Is the node out of CPU/memory?
Are there PersistentVolumeClaims pending?

bashkubectl describe nodes
kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>
4. Check for Deadlocks or Infinite Loops

Does the job seem to be in an infinite loop?
Is it waiting for an external resource that never responds?
Are there missing environment variables or configuration?

5. Verify Network Connectivity
For jobs that communicate with external services:
bash# Exec into the pod
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- sh
# Then test connectivity
ping <service>
curl <endpoint>
6. Common Solutions

Force job termination:
bashkubectl delete job <job-name> -n <namespace>

Fix timeouts:
Add .spec.activeDeadlineSeconds to your job specification to automatically terminate after a specific time
Implement retry logic:
Use .spec.backoffLimit to control retry attempts
Check application exit conditions:
Ensure your application code properly exits when complete
Review the code:
Look for issues in signal handling, thread management, or clean termination
Check dependency services:
Verify that all external services the job depends on are functioning

